    "title": "Ferrari's First Electric Vehicle Defies Criticism with Strong Sales",
    "category": "business",
    "categoryLabel": "Business",
    "icon": "💰",
    "date": "2026-07-29",
    "readTime": "7 min",
    "topic": "grandesempresas",
    "slug": "ferrari-s-first-electric-vehicle-defies-criticism-with-stron",
    "dek": "Despite skepticism and backlash from some car enthusiasts, the Ferrari Luce, the iconic brand's debut electric vehicle, is proving to be a commercial success.",
    "trending": false,
    "image": "img/temas/ferrari-s-first-electric-vehicle-defies-criticism-with-stron.jpg",
    "videoUrl": "",
    "body": "Ferrari, a name synonymous with luxury sports cars, is making waves in the electric vehicle market with the introduction of its first all-electric model, the Ferrari Luce. While traditionalists and critics express doubts about Ferrari’s foray into electric mobility, early sales figures indicate that the Luce is performing well against market expectations, showcasing a robust demand for high-performance electric vehicles.\n\n## A Shift in Strategy\n\nThe launch of the Luce represents not just the entry of Ferrari into the electric segment but also a significant shift in strategy for the iconic brand. As global automotive trends increasingly tilt towards sustainability, manufacturers are pivoting to embrace electric mobility. Ferrari’s move reflects an acknowledgment of this trend, despite the brand's historical commitment to combustion engines and the visceral experiences they deliver.\n\nThe Luce’s success in the marketplace underscores the growing acceptance of EVs among consumers, even those loyal to high-performance brands. This evolving consumer mindset may suggest that the allure of a Ferrari extends beyond the roar of its engine to include advancements in technology and sustainability.\n\n## Battling Tradition and Expectations\n\nThe reaction from automotive enthusiasts has been mixed. Many view the electric Ferrari as a departure from the brand's storied lineage of gas-powered vehicles, leading to skepticism about how it could live up to the famed performance standards. Critics often question whether an electric vehicle can replicate the driving experience that enthusiasts associate with Ferrari.\n\nHowever, the initial sales success may counter this narrative. The Luce seemingly captivates not only traditional Ferrari fans but also a new demographic interested in electric performance. High-profile sports cars from other manufacturers have already demonstrated the capability of electric powertrains to deliver thrilling performance, raising expectations and interest in Ferrari’s electric model.\n\n## Implications for the Luxury Vehicle Market\n\nWith the Luce, Ferrari is not merely following the trend; it is actively shaping the future of luxury performance vehicles in an electric context. The response to the Luce may influence how other luxury manufacturers approach their electric vehicle offerings. If Ferrari's model continues to achieve commercial success, it could signal a broader acceptance of electric vehicles among high-end consumers, who typically prioritize performance and luxury features.\n\nMoreover, the Luce could inspire innovation within the sector as brands recalibrate to meet the expectations of a changing market. The electric segment’s anticipated growth presents opportunities for luxury manufacturers to redefine performance metrics that emphasize efficiency and sustainability without compromising excitement.\n\n## Ferrari's Future Electric Innovations\n\nAs Ferrari continues to develop its electric lineup, the success of the Luce will likely be scrutinized closely by both supporters and detractors. The brand's approach to integrating electric power with its legacy performance will be a significant focus moving forward.\n\nThe automotive landscape is evolving, and as legacy brands adapt to meet these changes, the acceptance and performance of vehicles like the Luce may continue to pave the way for future electric models in the luxury sector. What remains to be seen is how Ferrari balances its heritage with the demands of modern consumers, and whether the Luce will be the catalyst for further electric innovations in its lineup.\n\nOverall, the Luce is a critical indicator of both market trends and the shifting perceptions surrounding electric vehicles in the luxury segment, portraying how esteemed brands can pivot towards sustainability while still enjoying commercial prosperity.\n\n[publicidad]\n\n## The numbers behind \"strong sales\"\n\nThe vehicle is the Ferrari Luce, a five-seat EV priced at €555,000 (roughly $630,000) and largely designed by Jony Ive's firm LoveFrom. Ferrari hit its annual sales target of around 500 units in just two months, with roughly 20% of sales going to China. Ferrari's stock fell about 8% after the car's May 2026 reveal amid public criticism, including comparisons to a Nissan Leaf from former Ferrari chairman Luca Cordero di Montezemolo -- criticism the actual sales performance has since undercut.",
    "href": "categoria/business/ferrari-s-first-electric-vehicle-defies-criticism-with-stron.html",
    "sourceUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/29/sorry-haters-ferraris-first-ev-is-doing-just-fine/",
    "sourceTitle": "Sorry, haters. Ferrari’s first EV is doing just fine",
    "noindex": false,
    "correctionNote": "Corregido 2026-09-10: se agregaron las cifras concretas ausentes en la version original -- precio (555.000 euros / ~630.000 dolares), objetivo de ventas anual (~500 unidades, alcanzado en solo dos meses), participacion de China (~20% de las ventas), la caida de un 8% en la accion de Ferrari tras la presentacion en mayo, y el diseñador (Jony Ive / LoveFrom).",
