    "title": "NASA's Swift Boost: A Mission to Save a Satellite Hits a Snag",
    "category": "science",
    "categoryLabel": "Science & Space",
    "icon": "🚀",
    "date": "2026-07-30",
    "readTime": "8 min",
    "topic": "nasa",
    "slug": "nasa-s-swift-boost-a-mission-to-save-a-satellite-hits-a-snag",
    "dek": "A commercial servicing spacecraft racing to reboost NASA's aging Swift Observatory suffered a real, documented control failure in late July 2026 — and engineers are now working against an October deadline to save the mission.",
    "trending": false,
    "image": "img/temas/nasa-s-swift-boost-a-mission-to-save-a-satellite-hits-a-snag.jpg",
    "videoUrl": "",
    "body": "NASA's Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory, in orbit since 2004 and central to the study of gamma-ray bursts, is losing altitude faster than expected due to increased solar activity. To prevent an uncontrolled reentry, NASA contracted Katalyst Space to fly a servicing mission — Swift Boost — designed to dock with the aging observatory and push it back up to a stable 600-kilometer orbit.\n\n## The Mission So Far\n\nKatalyst's servicing spacecraft, named LINK, launched on July 3, 2026 aboard a Pegasus-XL rocket, air-launched from Northrop Grumman's Stargazer L-1011 aircraft. The plan called for LINK to rendezvous with Swift and perform the orbit-raising maneuver before Swift's altitude became too low to save.\n\n## A Real Setback, Not a Hypothetical One\n\nOn July 29, 2026, LINK experienced a serious control anomaly: NASA and Katalyst confirmed a multi-axis spin lasting 72 hours, with two of the spacecraft's three reaction wheels non-functional and only partial reaction-control-system (RCS) capability remaining. The spacecraft briefly lost communication with ground controllers before a system reset restored contact.\n\nDespite the malfunction, Katalyst has said the mission remains active. The company has outlined a recovery plan using electric propulsion thrusters to stabilize LINK and continue toward a rendezvous with Swift, stating: \"The mission remains active and we continue to believe that with these changes, LINK has a viable path to rendezvous with Swift.\"\n\n## Racing the Clock\n\nThe stakes are time-sensitive. Swift's orbit is projected to decay to a critically low 300 kilometers by October 2026 — the point past which a reboost would no longer be possible, and the observatory would be lost to reentry. Whether LINK can complete its repairs and reach Swift before then remains an open question that NASA, Katalyst, and the astrophysics community are watching closely, given Swift's ongoing role in tracking cosmic gamma-ray bursts and other high-energy phenomena.",
    "href": "categoria/science/nasa-s-swift-boost-a-mission-to-save-a-satellite-hits-a-snag.html",
    "sourceUrl": "https://www.space.com/space-exploration/launches-spacecraft/nasa-launched-this-spacecraft-to-save-a-satellite-now-it-needs-saving-too",
