    "title": "Boxer Hannah Rapp Dies at 26 After Being Struck While Cycling in Texas",
    "category": "sports",
    "categoryLabel": "Sports",
    "icon": "⚽",
    "date": "2026-07-31",
    "readTime": "8 min",
    "topic": "hannahrapp",
    "slug": "tragic-end-to-promising-boxing-career-remembering-hannah-rap",
    "dek": "A man has been arrested and charged in the July 18 death of WBC featherweight title challenger Hannah Rapp in Brazos County, Texas; the charges are pending and he has not been convicted in connection with them.",
    "trending": false,
    "image": "img/temas/tragic-end-to-promising-boxing-career-remembering-hannah-rap.jpg",
    "videoUrl": "",
    "body": "Hannah Rapp, a 26-year-old professional boxer who had challenged for a world title weeks earlier, died on July 18, 2026, after being struck while riding her bicycle in Brazos County, Texas, according to the <a href=\"https://abc13.com/story/boxer-hannah-rapp-struck-car-riding-bicycle-dies-26-brazos-county-sheriffs-office-says/19538321/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Brazos County Sheriff's Office, cited by ABC13</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.kbtx.com/2026/07/19/hannah-hanarchy-rapp-champion-boxer-killed-brazos-county-crash/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">KBTX</a>. A man, Charles Medina, 31, was arrested at the scene and has since been charged in connection with Rapp's death. The charges remain pending. The reports reviewed through July 28 did not identify a trial date, and Medina has not been convicted in connection with these charges.\n\n## Boxing Career\n\nRapp, who fought under the nickname \"Hanarchy,\" was undefeated as a professional at 8-0-1 heading into a June 13, 2026, bout against WBC women's featherweight champion Tiara Brown in Orlando, Florida, according to the <a href=\"https://wbcboxing.com/en/tiara-brown-and-hannah-rapp-to-clash-for-the-iconic-wbc-juneteenth-belt-in-orlando-florida/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">World Boxing Council</a>. Brown retained her title, outpointing Rapp by decision — Rapp's first loss as a professional, not her professional debut. Rapp was originally from Yorktown, Indiana, ran track at Purdue University, and had relocated to Texas in 2024, working full-time as a fire and life-safety inspector at Texas A&M University while she trained, according to <a href=\"https://www.texasmonthly.com/culture/remembering-boxer-hannah-rapp/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Texas Monthly</a>.\n\n## The July 18 Incident\n\nAccording to the Brazos County Sheriff's Office, as reported by ABC13 and KBTX, Rapp was cycling on a rural highway in Brazos County, near College Station, shortly before 10 a.m. on Saturday, July 18, 2026, when investigators say a passing vehicle stopped, reversed and struck her. She was taken to a hospital, where she died. She had been riding with another cyclist, who was not struck. Investigators described it as an isolated incident, not a broader public-safety threat.\n\n## Initial Charge: Manslaughter\n\nMedina was arrested at the scene and is accused of striking Rapp with his vehicle; he was initially charged with manslaughter, with bond set at $250,000, according to ABC13 and KBTX reporting published July 18-19, 2026. As of that reporting, Medina had not entered a plea.\n\n## Additional Charge: Aggravated Assault\n\nOn July 23, 2026, <a href=\"https://www.kbtx.com/2026/07/23/man-accused-killing-boxer-hannah-rapp-faces-an-additional-charge/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">KBTX reported</a> that prosecutors had filed an additional charge against Medina, aggravated assault with a deadly weapon, with bond on that charge set at $500,000. According to <a href=\"https://wtaw.com/charles-medina-facing-new-charges-after-killing-hannah-rapp/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WTAW</a>, court documents allege the additional charge concerns Medina's use of his vehicle toward the other cyclist who was riding with Rapp when he reversed. Together, the two charges directly tied to this case carried combined bonds of $750,000 — $250,000 for manslaughter and $500,000 for aggravated assault with a deadly weapon. At Medina's first court appearance on July 28, 2026, both <a href=\"https://www.kbtx.com/2026/07/28/bonds-set-over-1m-man-charged-killing-rising-boxing-star/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">KBTX</a> and <a href=\"https://wtaw.com/charles-medina-makes-first-court-appearance-since-death-of-hannah-rapp/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WTAW</a> reported a higher combined total, $1,229,000, across six charges; the July 28 reports described the additional charges as separate, earlier pending or outstanding matters that predate Rapp's death, without detailing each one further here.\n\nAt the July 28 hearing, according to KBTX, Judge John Brick denied a request to lower Medina's bonds; Assistant District Attorney Jessica Escue argued the case involved \"a vile charge against innocent civilians,\" and defense attorney Sarah Wilkinson said the bonds as set made it impossible for Medina to be released. WTAW reported that Judge John Brick cited Medina's prior pending cases and public-safety concerns when denying the request to reduce bond. A status hearing on the manslaughter charge was scheduled for roughly 60 days later, pending a grand jury indictment. The reports reviewed through July 28 did not identify a trial date, and Medina had not entered a plea on any of the charges as of that reporting.\n\n## Community Tributes\n\nWBC president Mauricio Sulaimán said, according to ABC13: \"The loss of Hannah Rapp leaves us heartbroken. She was an exceptional boxer, but above all, an invaluable member of our boxing family.\" Tiara Brown, who beat Rapp in their June 13 fight, said, according to Texas Monthly: \"I'm just gonna be honest, I've cried a lot.\" Coach Carl Perry and friend Patsy Gyllin remembered Rapp's positive attitude and said her life and character extended far beyond her identity as a fighter, according to Texas Monthly. Rapp's mother started an online petition, \"Hannah's Law,\" calling on the Texas Legislature to expand a judge's authority to deny bond to defendants with a documented pattern of dangerous behavior; <a href=\"https://www.kbtx.com/2026/07/28/man-charged-hannah-rapps-death-faces-bond-hearing-tuesday-hannahs-law-petition-tops-10000-signatures/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">KBTX reported it had surpassed 10,000 signatures</a> as of July 28, 2026.\n\n[publicidad]",
    "href": "categoria/sports/tragic-end-to-promising-boxing-career-remembering-hannah-rap.html",
    "sourceUrl": "https://www.espn.com/boxing/story/_/id/49483096/boxer-hannah-rapp-death-texas-medina",
    "sourceTitle": "How Hannah Rapp's life and bright boxing future ended on a Texas road",
    "noindex": false,
    "noindexReason": "",
    "dateModified": "2026-09-06",
    "correctionNote": "Corrected Sep 6, 2026: the previous version did not cite its sources adequately and described the case only in general terms. This version corrects the date of the incident (July 18, not 19), adds the specific charges and bond amounts with the date and outlet reporting each one, separates the $750,000 in bonds tied directly to Rapp's case from separate earlier pending charges, and distinguishes the pending accusations against the arrested man from any judicially established fact — he has not been convicted in connection with these charges. It also corrects the record of Rapp's June 13 fight, which was a world-title challenge after an undefeated 8-0-1 start, not her professional debut. Updated again Sep 6, 2026 to use more precise, time-bound language about the pending case status and to condense the bond details.",
