    "title": "Massive Attack Members Barred from Re-entering Singapore After Flag Display",
    "category": "entertainment",
    "categoryLabel": "Entertainment",
    "icon": "🎬",
    "date": "2026-08-01",
    "readTime": "7 min",
    "topic": "massiveattack",
    "slug": "massive-attack-members-face-permanent-ban-from-singapore-aft",
    "dek": "Singapore police and media regulators say Robert Del Naja and Grant Marshall breached performance licence conditions by displaying a Palestinian flag on stage; Massive Attack says its members were detained and questioned, with some hotel rooms searched and passports temporarily confiscated.",
    "trending": false,
    "image": "img/temas/massive-attack-members-face-permanent-ban-from-singapore-aft.jpg",
    "videoUrl": "",
    "body": "Two members of the British band Massive Attack, Robert Del Naja and Grant Marshall — known as Daddy G — have been barred from re-entering Singapore after displaying a Palestinian flag on stage at the end of their July 29, 2026, concert at The Star Theatre, according to a <a href=\"https://www.police.gov.sg/Media-Hub/News/2026/08/20260731_joint_spf_imda_statement_on_incident_relating_to_british_band_massive_attack\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">joint statement</a> issued by the Singapore Police Force and the Infocomm Media Development Authority (IMDA) on July 31, 2026. One of the two also shouted \"Free Palestine\" during the performance, the statement says.\n\n## The Official Account\n\nAccording to the joint SPF-IMDA statement, the event organiser had previously acknowledged licence conditions specifying that \"there shall be no display of flags in support of any causes.\" Authorities said the display could \"potentially harm the racial and religious harmony in Singapore\" and urged the public to \"refrain from importing foreign politics.\" Citing Section 3 of the Foreign National Emblems (Control of Display) Act 1949 and Section 16 of the Public Order Act, the statement said Del Naja and Marshall each received a \"stern warning\" and would be \"banned from re-entering Singapore.\" Separately, IMDA said it \"will not grant any future application for performances by the band in Singapore\" — a restriction on the group's future bookings that is distinct from the personal re-entry ban on the two men. The statement does not describe either measure as permanent, does not name a duration, and does not indicate that either man was criminally charged, fined or imprisoned.\n\n## The Band's Account\n\nMassive Attack disputed how the incident was handled in a public statement first reported by <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/massive-attack-band-says-singapore-police-detained-questioned-them-after-concert-2026-08-03/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Reuters</a>, with local and international outlets including <a href=\"https://www.asiaone.com/singapore/massive-attack-respond-singapore-ban-over-palestine-flag-surprised-and-disappointed\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AsiaOne</a> and <a href=\"https://www.thenationalnews.com/arts-culture/music-stage/2026/08/04/uk-band-massive-attack-banned-from-singapore-over-palestinian-flag-at-concert/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The National</a> carrying the band's own words. The band said its members were \"isolated and separately questioned\" by police, and that some had hotel rooms searched and passports temporarily confiscated, according to <a href=\"https://www.thenationalnews.com/arts-culture/music-stage/2026/08/04/uk-band-massive-attack-banned-from-singapore-over-palestinian-flag-at-concert/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The National</a>. \"We were surprised and disappointed,\" the band said, adding that they \"did not imagine that merely holding up the flag of a sovereign state recognised by 157 countries would violate any law.\" The band described the display as an impromptu act of solidarity, saying fans in the crowd had also spontaneously joined in showing support for Palestinians, and closed its statement by invoking \"universal human rights & freedom of expression.\" VexlowHQ could not access Reuters' article directly to verify its exact wording, and has not independently verified the band's account of the searches and passport confiscation beyond this reporting.\n\nAccording to <a href=\"https://www.thenationalnews.com/arts-culture/music-stage/2026/08/04/uk-band-massive-attack-banned-from-singapore-over-palestinian-flag-at-concert/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The National</a>, visual projections during the show also referenced the conflicts in Gaza, Sudan and Ukraine, as well as the recent Iran war — the flag moment capped a set that touched on several international conflicts, not only the war in Gaza.\n\n## A Documented Pattern\n\nThe Singapore incident is not the first time Del Naja has tied his work to Palestine and other conflicts. According to <a href=\"https://news.artnet.com/art-world/massive-attack-robert-del-naja-art-music-activism-2596014\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Artnet News</a>, Del Naja has sold his artwork on a nonprofit basis for years, directing proceeds to NGO projects supporting Ukraine, Palestine and food security, part of a broader activist record for the band that Artnet traces back to the Gulf and Balkan wars of the early 1990s.\n\n## Timeline\n\n- July 29, 2026: Massive Attack performs at The Star Theatre in Singapore; Del Naja and Marshall display a Palestinian flag at the close of the show, and one shouts \"Free Palestine.\"\n- July 31, 2026: The Singapore Police Force and IMDA issue a joint statement announcing stern warnings for both men, a personal ban on their re-entry to Singapore, and IMDA's refusal to approve future performances by the band in the country.\n- August 2-3, 2026: Massive Attack issues a public statement describing the questioning and defending the flag display; Reuters and other outlets, including AsiaOne and The National, report the statement on August 3.\n\n[publicidad]",
    "href": "categoria/entertainment/massive-attack-members-face-permanent-ban-from-singapore-aft.html",
    "sourceUrl": "https://variety.com/2026/music/news/massive-attack-duo-banned-singapore-palestinian-flag-1236825534/",
    "sourceTitle": "Massive Attack Duo Banned From Singapore Over Palestinian Flag Display",
    "dateModified": "2026-09-06",
    "correctionNote": "Corrected Sep 6, 2026: the previous version incorrectly identified Elvis Costello — who has no connection to Massive Attack — as a founding member; the band member involved is Grant Marshall (\"Daddy G\"). This version adds the concert date and the official Singapore Police Force/IMDA statement, the band's own public response, and distinguishes the personal re-entry ban on the two men from IMDA's separate refusal to approve future performances by the group. It also removes the unsupported description of the measure as \"permanent\" — the official statement does not use that word or specify a duration.",
