    "title": "SpaceX Closes $60 Billion Acquisition of AI Coding Startup Cursor",
    "category": "ai",
    "categoryLabel": "AI",
    "icon": "🤖",
    "date": "2026-08-16",
    "readTime": "7 min",
    "slug": "spacex-completes-acquisition-of-ai-coding-startup-cursor",
    "dek": "The all-stock deal for Cursor's parent company, Anysphere, closed August 15, 2026, after SpaceX exercised an option to acquire it -- aimed at strengthening Grok's coding abilities, not rocket software.",
    "image": "img/temas/spacex-completes-acquisition-of-ai-coding-startup-cursor.jpg",
    "videoUrl": "",
    "trending": false,
    "body": "## What Happened, When, According to Whom\n\nSpaceX completed its $60 billion, all-stock acquisition of Cursor -- the AI coding assistant made by Anysphere -- on August 15, 2026, according to <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/15/spacex-officially-closes-its-cursor-acquisition/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TechCrunch</a>. Cursor becomes a wholly owned subsidiary of SpaceX. The deal was formally announced on June 10, 2026, after SpaceX had signaled interest as early as April, according to <a href=\"https://www.cbsnews.com/news/spacex-cursor-60-billion-ai-acquisition/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CBS News</a>, and closed on the Q3 2026 timeline SpaceX had projected.\n\n## Why SpaceX Wanted Cursor\n\nThis is not, primarily, an aerospace-software story. Per CBS News, the deal's stated purpose is strengthening the coding capabilities of Grok, the AI model made by xAI -- which SpaceX itself acquired in a $1.25 trillion merger on February 2, 2026, according to <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/02/elon-musk-spacex-acquires-xai-data-centers-space-merger/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TechCrunch's report on that deal</a>. SpaceX and Cursor have jointly trained an AI model not yet released, and Cursor is expected to use xAI's data center in Memphis, Tennessee, for future development. CBS News frames the rationale as competitive: matching OpenAI and Anthropic in frontier AI coding tools.\n\n## What Cursor's CEO Said\n\nCursor CEO Michael Truell announced the deal in June with a specific phrase worth noting: \"We are excited to share that SpaceX has exercised their option to acquire Cursor in an all-stock transaction,\" he said, adding it would \"advance our frontier AI capabilities.\" The word \"option\" indicates SpaceX and Cursor had a pre-existing option agreement rather than an acquisition negotiated from scratch -- a detail largely absent from other coverage of the closing.\n\n## Timeline\n\n- Feb 2, 2026: SpaceX acquires xAI in a $1.25 trillion merger.\n- April 2026: SpaceX first signals interest in acquiring Cursor.\n- June 10, 2026: Deal formally announced; Truell confirms SpaceX exercised its acquisition option.\n- Aug 15, 2026: Acquisition closes; Cursor becomes a SpaceX subsidiary.\n\n## What This Article Does Not Claim\n\nNo source found in reporting on this deal ties Cursor's tools to SpaceX's rocket or Starship software, contrary to what the previous version of this article implied. That framing has been removed. See also VexlowHQ's <a href=\"cursor-targets-growth-in-india-with-local-pricing-amid-space.html\">coverage of Cursor's India expansion</a>, announced in the weeks before this deal closed.",
    "sourceUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/15/spacex-officially-closes-its-cursor-acquisition/",
    "sourceTitle": "SpaceX officially closes its Cursor acquisition",
    "href": "categoria/ai/spacex-completes-acquisition-of-ai-coding-startup-cursor.html",
    "correctionNote": "Resuelto de Lote #7-20 (clasificacion A, reescrito y verificado durante auditoria previa; estaba en noindex solo en espera de aprobacion global de fase, que ya no aplica bajo la auditoria global continua). Indexado el 2026-09-09.",
